<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · river</p><h1 class="enc-title">River Tay</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The River Tay is the longest river in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, about 190 km long, and the largest in the United Kingdom by volume of water. It flows from the western Highlands through Loch Tay and past <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a> to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/firth-of-tay">Firth of Tay</a> at Dundee. Its valley was a frontier of Roman campaigns and a heartland of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/picts">Picts</a>, with Scone, the inauguration place of Scottish kings, near its banks.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/river-tay.jpg" alt="River Tay" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Dudesleeper at English Wikipedia · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:St._Matthew%27s_Church_and_Smeaton%27s_Bridge.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
